  "title": "How a Bill Becomes a Law",
  "canvas": { "aspect_ratio": "16:9", "background_color": "white" },
  "transcript": "A bill is first introduced in Congress by a sponsor in the House or Senate. The bill is then sent to committee review, where legislators study and debate it. If the committee approves, the bill proceeds to a floor vote before the full chamber. When passed, the bill is sent to the President for signature. Once signed, the bill becomes a law.",
